{"make_slug": "jeep", "name": "Jeep", "country_of_origin": "United States", "years_active_au_start": 1946, "years_active_au_end": "Present", "currently_sold_au": 1, "local_manufacturer": 1, "overview": "The original 4x4 brand, with rich Australian heritage including post-war CKD assembly of Willys and CJ Jeeps in Sydney and Brisbane, through to modern Wrangler, Cherokee, and Grand Cherokee.", "australian_history": "Willys-Overland Jeeps first arrived in Australia during the Second World War with Allied forces. In the post-war era, Willys Motors Australia and Pressed Metal Corporation in Enfield, Sydney assembled CKD kits of the CJ-3B, CJ-5, and Willys utility wagons. In the 1960s and 1970s, Kaiser Jeep and AMC assembled Jeep CJ-6, CJ-7, and Gladiator pickups in Rocklea, Brisbane. The modern retail resurgence began in 1994 when Chrysler Jeep Australia reintroduced the Cherokee (XJ), offering unbeatable value 4.0-litre inline-six capability. The Wrangler (TJ, JK, JL) became Australia's ultimate lifestyle off-roader, while the Grand Cherokee (WJ, WK2) achieved massive towing popularity across the nation.", "models": [{"id": 371, "make_slug": "jeep", "make_name": "Jeep", "model_slug": "grand-cherokee", "name": "Grand Cherokee", "vehicle_type": "Large SUV", "years_sold_au_start": 1996, "years_sold_au_end": "Present", "locally_built": 0, "overview": "The American flagship that revolutionized Australia's large SUV market. Across five generations (ZJ, WJ, WK, WK2, and WL), the Grand Cherokee blended authentic Jeep trail capability with luxury appointments, muscular HEMI V8s, efficient 3.0L diesels with 3,500kg towing, and the earth-shattering 522kW Grand Cherokee Trackhawk.", "generation_count": 3, "engine_count": 4, "badge_count": 4, "manual_count": 4, "hero_image": "media/pictures/jeep/grand-cherokee/jeep_grand-cherokee_hero_1_Jeep_Grand_Cherokee_WL_4xe_IMG_8479_cropped_.jpg"}, {"id": 372, "make_slug": "jeep", "make_name": "Jeep", "model_slug": "wrangler", "name": "Wrangl